extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Styling | Text"
	- pagedescription = "The font and text sizes in a Tablechart chart."

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Text
				p.lead The font and the sizes of the text in a chart.

	+section("Font")
		p All text in a chart is HTML, so it uses the font of the page. If you want to use another font in a chart, set #[code font-family] on it.

	+section("Sizes")
		p #[code --tablechart-font-size] sets the size of all text in a chart: #[code 1em] by default. The other sizes are relative to it.

		+style.
			.sizes {
				--tablechart-value-size: 2em;
				--tablechart-name-size: 0.75em;
			}

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure.sizes(data-chart="column")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Spring', '58'], ['Summer', '72'], ['Autumn', '70'], ['Winter', '91']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		table
			thead
				tr
					th Variable
					th Sets
			tbody
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-font-size
					td All text in a chart: 1em by default.
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-name-size
					td All names, under the bars and beside the lines: 0.95em by default.
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-value-size
					td The numbers on the bars: 1.3 times the names by default.
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-centre-size
					td The number in the middle of a donut: 1.6em by default.
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-caption-align
					td The alignment of the title: left by default.
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-caption-gap
					td The space under the title: 0.5em by default.

	+section("Text colours")
		p By default, all text in a chart has the text colour of the chart. If you want to give some of it another colour, set one of these variables.

		table
			thead
				tr
					th Variable
					th Sets
			tbody
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-name-color
					td The names of the rows, under the columns or beside the bars
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-value-color
					td The numbers on and after the bars, not inside them
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-axis-text-color
					td The numbers and titles along the axes. See #[a(href=url('/styling/axes.html')) Axes and gridlines].

	+section("Numbers inside bars or columns")
		p A number #[em inside] a bar or column needs a contrast of at least 4.5:1 with it, as text does. Tablechart aims for 5:1, so the number is easier to read.
		p By default, a number has the background colour of the chart, so it looks cut out of the bar, or the text colour of the chart:

		table
			thead
				tr
					th
					th Number on a dark bar
					th Number on a light bar
			tbody
				tr
					td Light background
					td The background colour
					td The text colour
				tr
					td Dark background
					td The text colour
					td The background colour
				tr
					td Set with
					td: code --tablechart-value-on-dark
					td: code --tablechart-value-on-light

		p Tablechart measures the background colour. See #[a(href=url('/styling/background.html')) Background].
		p For each number, Tablechart takes the one of these two colours that has more contrast with its bar, its part of a stack or its step of a waterfall. If that has less than 5:1, the number gets to be black or white instead, whichever reaches 5:1. On a middle colour, where neither does but both reach 4.5:1, it gets to be white, because white is easier to read there. If the colours of the page change, such as from light to dark mode, the numbers get their colour again.
		p If you want other colours, such as those of your brand, set #[code --tablechart-value-on-dark] for the numbers on dark bars, and #[code --tablechart-value-on-light] for the numbers on light bars. They replace the two default colours. The contrast is checked in the same way: if your colour has less than 5:1 with a bar, the number on that bar gets to be black or white, to achieve better contrast. If you want another goal than 5:1, set #[code --tablechart-value-contrast], such as #[code 7] for 7:1. With APCA, the goal is Lc 60, and the setting is an Lc value, such as #[code 75]. See #[a(href=url('/accessibility.html#contrast')) Contrast].

		style.
			.demo:has(.inside-choice) {
				color-scheme: light;
			}
			.inside-choice {
				color: #003646;
				--tablechart-color-1: #003646;
				--tablechart-color-2: #047b74;
				--tablechart-color-3: #3f8dbf;
				--tablechart-color-4: #b07d1e;
				--tablechart-color-5: #a87264;
			}

		.card
			.demo
				figure.inside-choice(data-chart="bar" data-chart-stack)
					figcaption
						b How do visitors rate each apple?
						br
						span % of votes
					table
						thead
							tr
								th Variety
								th Love it
								th Like it
								th Fine
								th Not keen
								th Dislike it
						tbody
							each row in [['Elstar', '34', '22', '18', '14', '12'], ['Jonagold', '28', '25', '20', '15', '12'], ['Granny Johnson', '18', '20', '24', '20', '18']]
								tr
									th= row[0]
									each value in row.slice(1)
										td= value
			p In this example, the text colour of the chart is navy, and nothing else is set. The numbers on dark bars have the measured background colour, the pink of the card, and the numbers on light bars have the navy of the text. The numbers on the navy parts are pink. On the teal parts, pink has less than 5:1, so the numbers there get to be white. On the blue, amber and brown parts, navy has less than 5:1, so the numbers there get to be black.

		p #[code --tablechart-value-align] sets where a number inside its bar goes: #[code start], where the bar starts, #[code center], or #[code end], where it ends. On a waterfall, a step down ends at the bottom. The number is as far from the start or end as the widest number is from the sides. Columns and bars use #[code end] by default, a waterfall #[code center].

		+style.
			.inside-start {
				--tablechart-value-align: start;
			}

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure.inside-start(data-chart="column" data-chart-labels="inside")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Spring', '58'], ['Summer', '72'], ['Autumn', '70'], ['Winter', '91']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

	+section("Smaller text on narrow charts")
		p With many columns on a narrow screen, smaller text lets more numbers fit. Short names under the chart help too: see #[a(href=url('/types/line.html#many-rows')) Many rows]. If the element around the chart is a container, #[code --tablechart-name-size] can use #[code clamp()] and #[code cqi], a share of the width of the container. The numbers are 1.3 times the name size, so they follow. In this example, the names and numbers are full size from 500px wide, and get smaller below that, down to 75%. The title keeps its size.

		+style.
			.text-box {
				container-type: inline-size;
			}
			.text-box > [data-chart] {
				--tablechart-name-size: clamp(0.7em, 3cqi, 0.95em);
			}

		p The buttons change the width of the example.

		.width-toggle.lang-toggle(role="group" aria-label="Width of the example")
			button(type="button" data-width="" aria-pressed="true") Wide
			button(type="button" data-width="min(360px, 60%)" aria-pressed="false") Narrow
		+chart(null, '1')
			.text-box
				figure(data-chart="column")
					figcaption: b Visitors per month
					table
						tbody
							each row in [['Jan', '41'], ['Feb', '38'], ['Mar', '52'], ['Apr', '61'], ['May', '74'], ['Jun', '88'], ['Jul', '93'], ['Aug', '90'], ['Sep', '77'], ['Oct', '63'], ['Nov', '49'], ['Dec', '45']]
								tr
									th= row[0]
									td= row[1]


		p Much smaller than that, text gets hard to read. Numbers that still do not fit are left out, and shown in the tooltip when you point at their row.

